From d4d106c88b3577be25db160e3fdfcd244fbcae6a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 08:41:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] The player information says the refresh rate
 that the display gets with VRR, measured

With a video at its own rate with VRR, the player information said the
refresh rate of the mode of the menus (359.999 Hz on a Samsung Odyssey
G60SD, 120 Hz on an LG C5) while the displays said 72 and 47 Hz:
the rate of the flips, which is what a display with
VRR on gets.

The flips are measured: VRR_VIDEO::CFlipRate takes the median of the
last 96 intervals between the out-fences of flips asked one slot apart
(the vblanks where a driver gives no out-fence; a missed slot is left
out, and the median keeps a late flip from moving it), and
ShownRefresh() says the rate asked for where the measure agrees with it
within 2 %, the measure where it does not, and the mode's until VRR is
confirmed and measured. The mode row, the notice of the playback mode
and the Dolby Vision page say "2560x1440 @ 71.928 Hz (VRR)"
(KODI::UTILS::VrrModeText()), the System page the same without the mark
(hades.out.modeplain: its VRR row says it, and its mode row has no room
for both), and the rate fit of the overview uses it. kodi.log says the
display's VRR minimum when VRR starts.

The VRR row (RowValue(), pure) says how many times each picture goes
below the display's VRR minimum, which the row of its VRR range shows
("On: each picture 3 times", Estuary #54307: a film at 23.976 cannot go
at 23.976 Hz on a display whose VRR starts at 40 or 48 Hz); "Off: Dolby
Vision changes the mode" while the Dolby Vision transport has the output
(#54308); "Off, as set in System > Display" where the settings hold
HDMI VRR off (#54309), which said "Held off for HDR10+ or Dolby Vision"
for every SDR video; "Not on: no mode at the video's rate" also with
the setting off, on a display that has no mode at the video's rate (the
G60SD at 359.999 Hz). "Held off for HDR10+" is left for HDR10+ on
Turing and Ampere. The help of "Video at its own rate with VRR" says
this, and what goes with VRR (Dolby Vision sent as HDR10, HDR10+ or SDR
too; native Dolby Vision changes the mode), in English, Spanish of
Spain, Argentina and Mexico. kodi.log says the measured rate at the end
of each video.
